    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "The conventional view of a recent \"Anthropocene\" fails to explain anomalous increases in atmospheric CO2 and CH4 that began thousands of years ago, which deviate from trends predicted by natural Earth-orbital cycles that had prevailed for hundreds of thousands of years.",
    "method": "This paper proposes that the Anthropocene began thousands of years ago, using paleoclimatic, archeological, and historical evidence to argue that anomalous Holocene increases in CO2 and CH4, which deviate from orbitally-predicted trends, were caused by early forest clearance and rice irrigation.",
    "task": "The task is to argue that the anthropogenic greenhouse era began thousands of years ago due to early agriculture, challenging the conventional view that it started with the industrial revolution by analyzing paleoclimatic and archeological evidence.",
